-- title:
--   Inertia p-characters at q≠ p are powers of the Kummer character
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a prime $q$ with $q \ne p$, and let $F$ be an intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q} \subseteq \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (the Mathlib algebraic closure) that is finite-dimensional and Galois over $\mathbb{Q}$. Write $G_q = \mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}_q}/\mathbb{Q}_q)$ for `primeLocalGaloisGroup q` and $r =$ `primeLocalToGlobal q` for the homomorphism $G_q \to \mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ obtained by restricting scalars to $\mathbb{Q}$ and then restricting to the normal subextension $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and let $I' \le G_q$ be the preimage under $r$ of the inertia subgroup at the valuation subring `primeLocalPlace q` of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ induced by the $q$-adic embedding (that is, of the image in $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ of the inertia subgroup inside the decomposition subgroup). Let $\chi : I' \to (\mathbb{Z}/p, \cdot)$ be a monoid homomorphism into the multiplicative copy of $\mathbb{Z}/p$, and assume $\chi(i) = 1$ for every $i \in I'$ whose image $r(i)$ lies in the fixing subgroup of $F$, i.e. acts trivially on $F$. Then there is a natural number $a$ with $\chi = (\,$`kummerCharacter p q hqp`$\,)^a$, where the Kummer character sends $\sigma \in I'$ to the class mod $p$ of the exponent $n$ for which $r(\sigma)$ carries a fixed $p$-th root $\alpha$ of $q$ in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ to $\zeta^n \alpha$, $\zeta$ a fixed primitive $p$-th root of unity.
--
--   This is the formal counterpart of the assertion that the group of continuous homomorphisms $I_{\mathbb{Q}_q} \to \mathbb{Z}/p$ ($q \ne p$) is cyclic, generated by the mod-$p$ tame character, realised concretely by Kummer theory on $q^{1/p}$: wild inertia is pro-$q$ and so is killed, and the $p$-part of the tame quotient is cyclic of order $p$. It feeds [`ExtCitation.exists_inertia_pCharacter_generator`](thm.html#ExtCitation.exists_inertia_pCharacter_generator), part of the local input at the auxiliary primes $q$ in the Taylor–Wiles argument.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ExtCitation_exists_eq_kummerCharacter_pow.lean

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ExtCitation_InertiaKummerCharacter

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
